    "title" : "OUTRAGED! by Kurt Gray, FRD senior fellow",
    "body" : "<div class=\"sqs-layout sqs-grid-12 columns-12\" data-layout-label=\"Post Body\" data-type=\"item\" id=\"item-6797e64a6f1c7375ce2881aa\"><div class=\"row sqs-row\"><div class=\"col sqs-col-12 span-12\"><div class=\"sqs-block website-component-block sqs-block-website-component sqs-block-html html-block\" data-block-css=\"[&quot;https://definitions.sqspcdn.com/website-component-definition/static-assets/website.components.html/cc58a320-ef63-4275-ac16-b0b276e029f1_983/website.components.html.styles.css&quot;]\" data-block-scripts=\"[&quot;https://definitions.sqspcdn.com/website-component-definition/static-assets/website.components.html/cc58a320-ef63-4275-ac16-b0b276e029f1_983/website.components.html.visitor.js&quot;]\" data-block-type=\"1337\" data-definition-name=\"website.components.html\" data-sqsp-block=\"text\" data-website-component-id=\"9433049cf3c499f009cf\" id=\"block-9433049cf3c499f009cf\"><div class=\"sqs-block-content\"><div class=\"sqs-text-block-container\">\n  \n  <div class=\"sqs-html-content\" data-sqsp-text-block-content><p class=\"\" style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\">Kurt Gray, one of FRD\u2019s senior fellows, has a new book out: <a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Outraged-Morality-Politics-Common-Ground/dp/0593317432/\" target=\"_blank\"><em>OUTRAGED! Why We Fight About Morality and Politics and How to Find Common Ground</em></a>. I have followed Gray\u2019s work for a while, and had a <a href=\"https://youtu.be/9mbQ0ul9Jo4?si=FNB3QuxVRsPp48I5\" target=\"_blank\">podcast conversation</a> with him before this book was written. In this volume he goes into detail and provides a multifaceted argument for harm-based morality that lies at the heart of our political and religious differences. In the past I accepted the moral foundations theory of Jonathan Haidt as seminal, but Gray\u2019s research and argumentation made me a convert to his ideas about ingroup moral concerns related to outgroups and their competing ideologies, beliefs, and sacred values. Be sure to visit Gray\u2019s <a href=\"https://outragedbook.com/\" target=\"_blank\">website</a> for the volume for additional reader information. Here\u2019s a description of the volume:</p><p class=\"\" style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\"><br>It\u2019s easy to assume that liberals and conservatives have radically different moral foundations. In Outraged, Kurt Gray showcases the latest science to demonstrate that we all have the same moral mind\u2014that everyone\u2019s moral judgments stem from feeling threatened or vulnerable to harm.<br><br>We all care about protecting ourselves and the vulnerable. Conflict arises, however, when we have different perceptions of harm. We get outraged when we disagree about who the \u201Creal\u201D victim is, whether we\u2019re talking about political issues, fights with our in-laws, or arguments on the playground.<br><br>In this fascinating and insightful tour of our moral minds, Gray tackles popular myths that prevent us from understanding ourselves and those around us. While it is commonly believed that our ancestors were apex predators, Gray argues that for the majority of our evolutionary history, humans were more hunted than hunter. This explains why our minds are hard-wired to perceive threats, and provides surprising insights on the scientific origins of our values and beliefs. Though we might think ourselves driven by objective reasoning, Gray unveils new research that finds our moral judgments are based on gut feelings rather than rational thought, and presents a compelling reminder that we are more alike than we might think.<br><br>Drawing on groundbreaking research, Gray provides a captivating new explanation for our moral outrage, and unpacks how to best bridge divides. If you want to understand the morals of the \u201Cother side,\u201D ask yourself a simple question\u2014what harms do they see?</p></div>\n\n\n  \n<style id=\"container-styles\">#block-9433049cf3c499f009cf {\n    \n    --stroke-style: none;--stroke-thickness: 6px;\n    \n      \n      \n      \n    \n\n\n\n  }\n\n  #block-9433049cf3c499f009cf .sqs-html-content {\n    \n    --tweak-text-block-padding: 6% 6% 6% 6%;\n  --tweak-text-block-padding: initial;\n}\n\n  #block-9433049cf3c499f009cf {\n      mix-blend-mode: var(--tweak-text-block-blend\n  );\n      border-radius: var(--tweak-text-block-radius);\n  }\n\n  \n  \n#block-9433049cf3c499f009cf {\n      --tweak-text-block-radius: 0px 0px 0px 0px;\n    }\n\n\n  \n  \n\n  #block-9433049cf3c499f009cf {\n    }\n\n</style>\n\n<style id=\"override-container-styles\">\n    @media screen and (max-width: 767px) {\n          \n            \n\n#block-9433049cf3c499f009cf {\n            \n            }\n        }\n    \n  \n    @media screen and (max-width: 767px) {\n          #block-9433049cf3c499f009cf .sqs-html-content {\n            \n            }\n        }\n    \n  @media screen and (max-width: 767px) {\n          \n          \n\n          \n          \n          \n        }</style>\n<style class=\"transform-overrides-vars\">\n  \n</style>\n\n\n\n\n</div>\n</div></div></div></div></div>",
